10:46:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Electric cars are often cheaper to operate because electric motors are far more efficient than gasoline engines. An EV may convert roughly 75–90% of electricity into motion, while a conventional internal-combustion car typically converts only about 20–30% of the fuel’s energy into motion. Home heating is a different comparison: - **Electric resistance heat is efficient at the house, but electricity can be expensive.** A baseboard heater turns nearly 100% of purchased electricity into heat. But burning natural gas in a modern furnace can still be cheaper per unit of heat because gas is often much cheaper than electricity, even after furnace losses. - **Electricity has upstream losses and costs.** Producing electricity at a power plant and transmitting it involves fuel, generation, grid, and delivery costs. In many places, using gas directly in a furnace is economically cheaper than burning fuel at a power plant, making electricity, then using it for resistance heat. - **Winter heating creates huge peak demand.** If every house used resistance electric heat, cold mornings and winter storms could require much larger power plants, transmission lines, and local distribution equipment. Building that capacity is expensive. - **Most homes already have heating equipment and fuel infrastructure.** Replacing a working gas or oil furnace, ducts, tanks, or boilers has an upfront cost. Energy decisions are not made only on operating cost. The important exception is **heat pumps**. They do not create heat mainly by resistance; they move heat from outdoors (or the ground) indoors. They commonly deliver about **2–4 units of heat for each unit of electricity** consumed. That can make them cheaper than gas or oil in many locations, especially with moderate electricity prices and cleaner grids. So the short answer is: EVs beat gasoline cars largely because motors are dramatically more efficient than engines. For homes, plain electric resistance heat often loses to direct gas heating on price and grid-demand grounds—but electric **heat pumps** are increasingly replacing fossil-fuel heating because they are much more efficient.
