10:46:16 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Not quite. Electricity is an **energy carrier**, not necessarily the original energy source. It can be made from gas, coal, nuclear, wind, solar, hydro, etc. Whether it is cheaper or more energy-efficient depends on what you are comparing. For cars: - A gasoline car burns fuel in a small engine that is inefficient, especially in stop-and-go driving. - An EV uses electricity in a highly efficient motor, can recover some braking energy, and avoids idling losses. - Even if some electricity came from a fossil-fuel power plant, a large modern power plant plus an EV can often use less fuel per mile than burning gasoline in millions of small engines. For home heat, compare two ways to use, say, natural gas: 1. **Burn it directly in a gas furnace** A modern furnace might deliver about 90–97% of the gas’s energy as heat indoors. 2. **Burn it at a power plant, send the electricity to a house, then use an electric resistance heater** The power plant may convert only roughly 35–60% of the fuel energy into electricity, and there are grid losses. A resistance heater is nearly 100% efficient once the electricity arrives, but it cannot recover the energy lost generating the electricity. So if the electricity is fossil-generated, direct gas heat can be more energy-efficient—and often cheaper—than resistance electric heat. For example, very roughly: 100 units of gas burned in a 95% furnace gives about **95 units of home heat**. If those 100 units go through a 40% efficient power plant, perhaps only around **38 units of electricity** reach the home, giving 38 units of resistance heat. The major exception is a **heat pump**. It uses electricity to *move* heat rather than make it directly. One unit of electricity can deliver 2–4 or more units of indoor heat, so it can beat direct gas heating even when the grid is partly fossil fueled. That is why electrification of heating generally means heat pumps, not ordinary electric baseboards.
