10:50:24 | INFO | 🤖 Target: “100% efficient” there means **at the appliance**: nearly every unit of electricity you buy becomes heat in the room. It does not mean it provides the cheapest heat. Cost effectiveness depends on how much the energy input costs and how much heat you get from it: - A resistance heater: 1 kWh of electricity → about 1 kWh of heat. - A heat pump: 1 kWh of electricity → often about 2–4 kWh of heat. - A gas furnace: 1 unit of gas → roughly 0.9–0.97 units of heat. So resistance heat is highly efficient at converting electricity to heat, but it can still be costly because electricity is often priced much higher per unit of energy than gas. A heat pump is usually more cost-effective because it produces—or, more accurately, delivers—several times as much heat from the same purchased kWh. Efficiency and cost are related, but they are not the same thing. A 100%-efficient device using expensive energy can cost more to run than a 90%-efficient device using cheap energy.
